      "publication_context": {
        "file": "503f15b855768ba3b9c360ee2c52f1872ff31217ef534adb18ee3ed70359c415",
        "current_lean_module": "import AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.StochasticProcesses.CarreDuChamp\n\n/-!\n# The algebraic Bakry--Émery interpolation bound\n\nFor a linear generator, the definition of the iterated carré du champ gives\n\n`2 Γ₂(f) = L Γ(f) - 2 Γ(f, Lf)`.\n\nCombining this identity with `CD(κ, ∞)` supplies the lower derivative bound\nused in the standard backward semigroup interpolation.  This file proves that\nalgebraic implication without assuming a concrete semigroup or its derivative\nformula.\n-/\n\nnamespace AutoSamplingTheory\nnamespace TechnicalLemmas\nnamespace StochasticProcesses\n\nopen CarreDuChamp\n\nvariable {X : Type*}\n\n/-- The fully expanded diagonal `CD(alpha, infinity)` inequality gives the\ngenerator expression used in backward interpolation.\n\nTo keep the public interface directly inspectable, the statement exposes\npositivity of `alpha`, every curvature quantifier, and the exact one-half\nnormalization of the iterated carré du champ in its signature. -/\ntheorem interpolationDerivative_lower_bound_of_expandedBakryEmery\n    (generator : (X → ℝ) →ₗ[ℝ] (X → ℝ))\n    {alpha : ℝ} (_halpha : 0 < alpha)\n    (hcurvature : ∀ (g : X → ℝ) (y : X),\n      alpha * carreDuChamp generator g g y ≤\n        (2 : ℝ)⁻¹ *\n          (generator (carreDuChamp generator g g) y -\n            carreDuChamp generator g (generator g) y -\n            carreDuChamp generator g (generator g) y))\n    (f : X → ℝ) (x : X) :\n    2 * alpha * carreDuChamp generator f f x ≤\n      generator (carreDuChamp generator f f) x -\n        2 * carreDuChamp generator f (generator f) x := by\n  have h := hcurvature f x\n  nlinarith\n\nend StochasticProcesses\nend TechnicalLemmas\nend AutoSamplingTheory\n",
        "toolchain": "302cd63c54178885b89e669f33b38f12f4dd7ae7e5cac537b3203e3768d8fb2b",
        "dependencies": "b83ca83b9cf7caa85fa8b023c2ff9a3fb7a7cce7e1c332e810c348535ec49c37",
        "source": {
          "url": "https://chewisinho.github.io/main.pdf#page=31",
          "edition": "Chewi, An Introduction to Sampling, edition 2026-08-09",
          "anchor": "Definitions 1.2.28–1.2.29, book pp. 18–19 / PDF pp. 30–31",
          "wording_status": "faithful paraphrase",
          "attribution": "Sinho Chewi defines Γ₂ and CD(α,∞); Samplinglib records their immediate pointwise algebraic consequence."
        },
        "statement": "For a linear generator satisfying CD(α,∞), the expression LΓ(f)−2Γ(f,Lf) is pointwise at least 2αΓ(f).",
        "formulae": [
          {
            "label": "curvature condition",
            "tex": "\\alpha\\Gamma(f)(x)\\le\\Gamma_2(f)(x)"
          },
          {
            "label": "expanded interpolation bound",
            "tex": "2\\alpha\\Gamma(f)(x)\\le L\\Gamma(f)(x)-2\\Gamma(f,Lf)(x)"
          }
        ],
        "assumptions": [
          "An arbitrary state type and a real-linear generator on real-valued observables.",
          "A certificate of CD(α,∞), including α>0 and the pointwise inequality αΓ(f)≤Γ₂(f).",
          "An arbitrary observable f and state x."
        ],
        "obligations": [
          {
            "id": "bakry-emery-curvature-interpolation-expression",
            "label": "Expand Γ₂ and derive the exact lower bound used in backward interpolation"
          }
        ],
        "lesson": {
          "declaration": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.StochasticProcesses.interpolationDerivative_lower_bound_of_expandedBakryEmery",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Curvature controls the interpolation derivative expression",
          "statement": "Let L be a real-linear generator and suppose it satisfies CD(α,∞): αΓ(f)≤Γ₂(f). Expanding Γ₂ gives the pointwise lower bound LΓ(f)−2Γ(f,Lf)≥2αΓ(f).",
          "formula": "\\Gamma_2(f)\\ge\\alpha\\Gamma(f)\\Longrightarrow L\\Gamma(f)-2\\Gamma(f,Lf)\\ge2\\alpha\\Gamma(f)",
          "assumptions": [
            "The state space X is arbitrary.",
            "L is a real-linear map from real-valued observables on X to real-valued observables on X.",
            "The curvature parameter α is strictly positive.",
            "For every observable g and point y, the exact expanded inequality αΓ(g,g)(y)≤(1/2)(LΓ(g,g)(y)−Γ(g,Lg)(y)−Γ(g,Lg)(y)) holds.",
            "No semigroup or differentiability assumption is present."
          ],
          "steps": [
